\DOC LOCAL_JADD_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_JADD_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal across one inlined
point-addition subroutine call, replacing the pre-call state assumptions by
post-call ones, including a new specification assumption about the subroutine's
result.

\DESCRIBE
The argument is an {int}: the index of the state variable for the new
(post-call) machine state. The tactic applies to a goal whose assumptions {A}
describe the machine state at the call site and whose conclusion is an
{eventually} predicate about state {s}n; the call's start state is that
previous state, and the resulting conclusion is the same {eventually} predicate
applied to a fresh state variable named by the argument index.
{
   A ?- eventually ... sn
   ================================================  LOCAL_JADD_TAC n'
   A' ?- eventually ... sn'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is obtained as follows. All assumptions of {A} mentioning
the old state are discharged and re-added, restated for the new state: the
program-loaded assumption, the register and memory-read assumptions (with those
describing the call's argument registers and return address dropped), the
instruction-pointer and stack-pointer assumptions set to the post-return
values, and a widened {MAYCHANGE} frame assumption relating the initial state
to the new state. One new universally quantified assumption is added, stating
the mathematical specification of the called routine in terms of memory reads
of the output buffers in the new state. Assumptions not referring to machine
state (arithmetic side conditions, earlier specification assumptions) are kept
unchanged.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of this {eventually} form with a full set of machine-state assumptions.

\ENDDOC
